---
title: "Books & sites"
description: "Where a book and a site each diverge from an ordinary page, and what you write differently for each."
---

One block model drives several outputs. The same `.tmd` you preview as a page becomes
a chapter or a site page unchanged; pointing the tool at a directory is what turns a
folder of documents into a book or a whole website.

## Books and a live table of contents

A **page** with `toc: true` gains a table of contents built from its heading anchors
and rebuilt live as you edit, with the current section highlighted as you scroll (a
scrollspy). On a wide screen it's a sticky sidebar; on a phone it appears inline,
after the article. A **book** has no such rail: its Chapters drawer lists every
chapter instead.

A **multi-chapter book** is a directory whose `_site.yml` lists `chapters:`:
their presence makes it a book (no `type:` needed), optionally grouped into
`part:`s. Taliesin numbers the chapters, builds the **Chapters** drawer, wires prev/next
navigation, and resolves cross-references across chapters. *These very docs are
such a book*: open **Chapters** and press <kbd>Cmd/Ctrl-K</kbd> to search.

Each chapter also shows its **prose length in words** in the drawer, so the cost of
opening a chapter is visible where that decision is
made. It is a word count and not a reading time on purpose: the count excludes fenced
code and math, so a code-heavy chapter is understated, and a minutes label would carry
that error into a promise about your time. Includes are resolved first, so a chapter
assembled from partials is measured at what you will actually read.

```yaml
title: "My Book"
chapters:
  - index.tmd
  - part: "Part One"
    chapters: [a.tmd, b.tmd]
```

The built pages are self-contained (framework CSS, JS and fonts are inlined or
root-relative), so a book copied anywhere still reads offline. There is nothing to
configure.

## Multi-page websites

Point either command at a **directory** and Taliesin treats it as a website
project. The directory needs one root config, `_site.yml`, which names the site
title, the navbar, and the footer; every `.tmd` under it (skipping `_`-prefixed
partials) becomes a page.

```yaml
title: "My Site"
favicon: favicon.svg
nav:
  - { text: Blog, href: blog.tmd }
  - { text: Projects, href: projects.tmd }
footer:
  left: "© 2026 Me"
  right:
    - { icon: github, href: "https://github.com/me" }
```

```sh
taliesin build   corpus/tech-blog     # static site -> _site/ (deployable)
taliesin preview corpus/tech-blog     # live site on http://127.0.0.1:4321
```

See the [configuration reference](../reference/configuration.tmd) for every key
(and the note on the native flat config schema).

`build` writes one `.html` per page (mirroring the input tree) and copies the
referenced assets, so the output folder deploys as-is. `preview` serves the same
site **live**: the navbar links navigate between pages (each a real
page load with its own websocket), and the page you are editing hot-reloads in
place. Intra-site `.tmd` links (including `other-page.tmd#anchor`) are rewritten to
their `.html` targets automatically.

To ship a website and a separate docs **book** as one deploy, build the book into a
subfolder of the site's output with `--out`, then link to it from the navbar:

```sh
taliesin build my-site                 # -> my-site/_site/
taliesin build docs --out my-site/_site/docs   # the book, nested at /docs
# navbar:  - { text: Docs, href: docs/index.html }
```

Config is **two levels, no cascade**: the root `_site.yml` sets project-wide
defaults and a page's own front matter overrides them. There is deliberately no
`_metadata.yml` directory-merge cascade.

Two front-matter blocks generate whole page sections so you do not hand-write the
chrome: `hero:` (a landing header) and `listing:` (an index that crawls a directory).
Both are page front matter, documented in full in the
[front-matter reference](../reference/frontmatter.tmd); the practical shapes follow.

### A landing header with `hero:`

`hero:` builds a centered landing band: an optional small uppercase `eyebrow:` kicker,
a big `headline:` (it falls back to the page `title:` when omitted), a supporting
`lead:` sentence, and a row of call-to-action buttons. Mark one button
`primary: true` to render it as the filled accent button; the rest are outlined.

```yaml
title: "My Project"
hero:
  eyebrow: "Open source"
  headline: "A faster way to publish"
  lead: "One source file, four output shapes, zero CDN."
  actions:
    - { text: "Get started", href: docs/index.html, primary: true }
    - { text: "Source", href: "https://github.com/me/proj" }
```

### An index of pages with `listing:`

`listing:` turns a page into an index. It crawls the directory named by `contents:`
(relative to the page) and emits a card per document there, pulling each card's image,
date, title, description, and categories from that document's own front matter. So a
blog index, a projects index, or a homepage's "recent posts" all generate from front
matter, and adding a post is just adding a file.

```yaml
title: "Blog"
listing:
  contents: posts
  type: grid
  max-items: 12
```

The knobs:

- `contents:` (required) is the directory to list, relative to this page.
- `type: grid` renders a card grid with cover images; omit it (or any other value) for
  a stacked list without images.
- `max-items:` caps how many cards appear.

There is no `sort:` key: a listing is always newest-first by `date`, which is what every
listing that ever set the key asked for.

## Themes

`theme:` points at a `.css`/`.scss` file or an installed `_extensions/<name>/`
bundle. There is no built-in mode name to select: both palettes ship in every
page and the reader's device picks between them. A theme is just a `:root` block
of CSS custom properties (`--tali-*`); the navbar, footer, callouts, diagrams, and
code all read those variables, so one small file restyles everything and
switching palettes just swaps the values: nothing re-renders. The full list of
`--tali-*` variables and how to author a theme is in [Theming](theming.tmd).

The `{{< include >}}` shortcode is documented in [Rich
writing](writing.tmd#reusing-a-partial), and its sibling `{{< input >}}` in
[Interactive and explorable documents](interactive.tmd#inputs-without-boilerplate-input).
